WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.960
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:07.150
TOM MCGRATH: Welcome to
"Cloud OnAir," live webinars

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:08.410
from Google Cloud.

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:10.520
We host webinars every Tuesday.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:12.340
My name is Tom
McGrath, and I'm joined

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:16.720
by Feng Lu, software engineer
from the Cloud Composer team.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:19.680
And today we'll be talking
about Cloud Composer,

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.660
and how it orchestrates
containers, and even more.

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:25.000
You can ask questions
anytime on the platform,

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.530
and we have Googlers on
standby to answer them.

00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:29.460
Let's get started.

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:30.460
So our agenda today.

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:33.560
We'll be looking at an overview
of Cloud Composer and Apache

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:34.390
Airflow.

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:36.910
We'll look at how to
use Cloud Composer.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:39.630
We'll also see what's
new in the GA release.

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:42.280
Feng will provide
an awesome demo.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:45.692
We'll also look at some DAG
examples of advanced concepts.

00:00:45.692 --> 00:00:47.650
And then from there are
next steps and roadmap,

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:50.570
and where to find out more.

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:52.560
So what is Cloud Composer?

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.550
As a starting point,
let's understand the fit

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:56.740
for Cloud Composer by
looking at the state

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:58.690
of the industry for a moment.

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:00.420
There's a lot of data today.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.440
I think we can all
agree about that.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:04.209
But how much data exactly?

00:01:04.209 --> 00:01:08.050
Well, IDC estimates 163
zettabytes of data--

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:10.870
that's 163 billion terabytes--

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:13.060
will exist by 2025.

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:14.210
That's a lot, right, Feng?

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:14.560
FENG LU: Yeah.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:15.310
TOM MCGRATH: Yeah.

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:18.760
So organizations are heavily
investing in public cloud

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:22.372
and currently are planning to
adopt public cloud strategies.

00:01:22.372 --> 00:01:23.830
But at the same
time, they're going

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:26.140
to continue with some of
their on-premises investments

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:27.140
as well.

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:29.260
So it's very clear that a
hybrid cloud strategy is

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:32.890
necessary in this environment.

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:34.620
With the need for
a hybrid strategy

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.080
and before Cloud Composer,
there were a few ways

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:39.600
of managing workflows
across systems or even

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.040
on the Google Cloud Platform.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:44.510
And I think it's fair to say
these were not the best at all.

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:45.510
FENG LU: No, not at all.

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:47.546
I wrote one of those stacks.

00:01:47.546 --> 00:01:48.420
It's a pain.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:48.930
TOM MCGRATH: Yeah, I mean--

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:50.100
FENG LU: It's not a
workflow solution.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:51.225
TOM MCGRATH: Yeah, exactly.

00:01:51.225 --> 00:01:53.910
Even just creating and
scheduling Cron jobs

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:55.410
and managing those,
or even going

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:58.380
to the other end of the
spectrum of creating

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:00.000
your own orchestration engine.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.330
It's just a lot of work with
a high cost of ownership.

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:02.990
FENG LU: Absolutely.

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:03.490
Yeah.

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:04.680
TOM MCGRATH: Yeah.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:06.840
So the Cloud
Composer team rightly

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:11.009
thought it should be easy to
create, schedule, and monitor

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:13.650
robust portable workflows
that work inside

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:16.230
and outside of
Google Cloud Platform

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:19.920
for a holistic
workflow lifecycle.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:21.690
So all these items
highlighted in blue

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:23.610
are key and critical
for a workflow.

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:25.950
It's more than a schedule
and just let it run,

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:27.330
or any one of these items.

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:28.860
It's all of them together.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:32.820
So we're aiming to provide
organizations a solution that

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:35.130
covers them all.

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:37.470
And that's where Cloud
Composer fits, which was just

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:39.290
made generally available.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:40.509
Nice work, Feng and team.

00:02:40.509 --> 00:02:41.300
FENG LU: Thank you.

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:42.287
TOM MCGRATH: Yep.

00:02:42.287 --> 00:02:44.370
And so it was just made
available inside of Google

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:45.476
Cloud Platform.

00:02:45.476 --> 00:02:47.100
And the short version
of Cloud Composer

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:50.670
is that it's managed
Apache Airflow.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.470
So why Cloud Composer?

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:56.790
End-to-end GCP integration,
orchestrate your full pipeline

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:58.650
through Cloud Composer.

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:01.170
And we want to make
sure that we supported

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:03.360
those hybrid and multi-cloud
environments that we're

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:05.640
seeing in the marketplace,
where these things are

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:08.250
just important to everyone.

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:10.380
We also want it to be very easy.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:12.090
Not have to wrangle
with lots of code.

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:15.690
Be able to leverage Python
to be able to orchestrate

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:18.600
these workflows, and not have to
write a lot of that code that's

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:20.080
necessary.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:21.780
But also keep it open source.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:23.430
We want people to
contribute and be

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:25.205
part of the larger community.

00:03:25.205 --> 00:03:25.830
FENG LU: Right.

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:26.490
Exactly.

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:28.510
TOM MCGRATH: Yeah.

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:31.420
So a common question
is, well, what's

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:34.150
the difference
between Airflow manage

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:35.830
and running it on some VMs?

00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:37.750
Well, what the team
has done is they've

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:41.150
created a seamless and
integrated experience with GCP.

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:42.790
So it's not standalone,
and it does not

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:44.790
feel like a standalone product.

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:47.960
Security, IAM, audit logging--

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:50.470
all those things are baked
in, so it acts and feels

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:52.850
like an actual Cloud product.

00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:55.540
It's also easy to use
when things go as not

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:58.070
expected within your workflows.

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:01.120
And so Stackdriver is integrated
for that exact reason.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:03.640
And we make it easy to
manage the deployment

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:05.560
with setting
environment variables

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:09.710
and maintaining your
Python packages.

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:12.080
Some of you might not be
familiar with Airflow,

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:13.580
or may have just heard of it.

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:15.480
So we're really
excited about it,

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:18.589
and we want you to see
why it's so exciting.

00:04:18.589 --> 00:04:21.950
So Airflow is currently
in incubating as an Apache

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:23.900
Software Foundation project.

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:25.650
It's actually been
around for a few years.

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:27.560
It's a leading
open-source package

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:29.870
to create and
schedule workflows.

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:33.157
So all the workflows in
it are code with Python,

00:04:33.157 --> 00:04:34.490
and they're highly approachable.

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:36.320
And you can do a lot
with it, including

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:37.630
some programmatic workflows.

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:40.720
And we'll actually look through
some quick, simple examples,

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:45.050
and you'll see how easy it is
to work with the code in Python

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:47.820
for Airflow.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:52.246
So the strengths of Airflow
in the open-source community--

00:04:52.246 --> 00:04:53.620
we wanted to bring
those together

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:55.870
with the strengths of
Google Cloud Platform.

00:04:55.870 --> 00:04:59.530
As you can see on the right-hand
side, Google Cloud Platform--

00:04:59.530 --> 00:05:01.960
we're great at running
infrastructure, creating

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:04.210
services, adding
layers of security,

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:07.360
auditability, and
containing costs.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:11.560
But there is no workflow
and orchestration solution.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:15.190
Airflow, on the other hand,
has really strong workflow

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:19.570
orchestration and connectors
inside and outside of GCP--

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:22.340
description language,
defined APIs, and so on.

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:24.070
So we wanted to
bring them together,

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:26.670
but also we wanted to contribute
back to the community.

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:31.270
And a great example of that
is the KubernetesPodOperator,

00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:32.930
which we'll cover
later on in the demo.

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:39.580
So within Airflow,
the key concepts.

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:42.620
All workflows are
graphs, series of tasks,

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:44.610
and a task that can
fit into a graph.

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:48.510
It can be as simple as a
Bash script to tell the time.

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:51.830
Of course, they get plenty
more complicated from there.

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:53.800
Graphs can have a
series of tasks,

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:55.670
and tasks are basically a step.

00:05:55.670 --> 00:05:59.900
For example, maybe I want to run
a SQL query or a BigQuery query

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:03.670
and export that to
Google Cloud Storage.

00:06:03.670 --> 00:06:06.900
There's also the
concept of a sensor--

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:09.570
a Boolean check on a step
that waits until something

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:10.630
has completed.

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:12.850
When true, it precedes.

00:06:12.850 --> 00:06:16.120
So we can orchestrate our
workflows very seamlessly

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:17.380
using these Airflow concepts.

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:23.330
With the DAG views
inside of Airflow,

00:06:23.330 --> 00:06:24.920
it makes it very
easy to understand

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:26.780
your workflow and
its properties,

00:06:26.780 --> 00:06:29.330
including any
bottlenecks or errors.

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:31.070
And really, what
this allows you to do

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:33.710
is chart a path to
higher reliability

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:35.960
of your orchestration workflows.

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:40.960
So in terms of additional
Airflow functionality--

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:42.040
connections.

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:44.530
There's all sorts of different
connections from Airflow

00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:46.180
into other systems.

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:47.830
We talked a lot about
logging and being

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:51.910
able to see what's going on
within Airflow, but also SLAs,

00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:54.900
whether or not you're
actually meeting those or not.

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:58.310
There's also the capability of
passing values between tasks.

00:06:58.310 --> 00:07:00.980
So lots of interesting and
advanced functionality.

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:04.670
And usually there's some
questions of, can Airflow do x?

00:07:04.670 --> 00:07:06.450
And usually the answer is yes.

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:07.376
FENG LU: Yeah.

00:07:07.376 --> 00:07:09.350
Although it often
means that you may

00:07:09.350 --> 00:07:11.720
need to-- either the
existing operators.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:16.192
You don't often you
have to try to come up

00:07:16.192 --> 00:07:17.150
with your own solution.

00:07:17.150 --> 00:07:19.100
That tends to be hard,
but it's still possible.

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:19.460
TOM MCGRATH: Yeah.

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:19.930
FENG LU: Yeah.

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:21.735
TOM MCGRATH: It's a
very flexible platform.

00:07:21.735 --> 00:07:22.180
FENG LU: Yep.

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:22.930
TOM MCGRATH: Yeah.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:26.470
In terms of Google
Cloud Platform,

00:07:26.470 --> 00:07:29.230
there's a core set of
Airflow support built

00:07:29.230 --> 00:07:32.830
in-- so Dataflow connectors,
BigQuery, Cloud Storage

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:33.970
to name a few.

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:35.650
And support is
actually expanding

00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:37.660
with each Airflow release.

00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:39.760
And this is happening
within the Airflow team,

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:41.590
but also within the
Google Cloud team.

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:46.830
So what about support
outside of GCP?

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:48.610
Really important.

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:51.450
So for example, I can
run Bash commands.

00:07:51.450 --> 00:07:54.060
I can call other public clouds.

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:56.490
I can connect into
JIRA, or even Slack,

00:07:56.490 --> 00:07:59.010
for example, to send
notifications and interact

00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:00.300
with those tools.

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:03.180
And there's so many
more under development.

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:07.170
So much support for
non-Google things,

00:08:07.170 --> 00:08:10.650
fully usable within Airflow,
and thus Cloud Composer as well.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.980
So in terms of some of the
new Cloud Composer features,

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:19.160
it's been made available
in a few new regions.

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:22.490
Stackdriver logging has been
very much expanded, which

00:08:22.490 --> 00:08:24.920
we'll see within the demo.

00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:28.610
There's also been the inclusion
of additional IAM roles.

00:08:28.610 --> 00:08:32.330
And then additional future
additions in Airflow.

00:08:32.330 --> 00:08:36.049
So the KubernetesPodOperator
brought in,

00:08:36.049 --> 00:08:39.740
Parquet source format, and
also location selection

00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:41.690
for Cloud Dataflow jobs.

00:08:41.690 --> 00:08:43.280
So more and more
functionality being

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:44.750
added in with Cloud
Composer, and I

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:46.751
think you can see that
with this GA release.

00:08:46.751 --> 00:08:47.334
FENG LU: Yeah.

00:08:47.334 --> 00:08:49.230
TOM MCGRATH: Yeah.

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:53.450
So what does using Cloud
Composer look like?

00:08:53.450 --> 00:08:57.560
So you create a Cloud Composer
environment within a project.

00:08:57.560 --> 00:09:00.890
Each environment is a managed
deployment of Apache Airflow,

00:09:00.890 --> 00:09:03.590
so you don't have to worry
about the installation

00:09:03.590 --> 00:09:06.262
and some other typical
administrative tasks.

00:09:06.262 --> 00:09:06.970
FENG LU: Exactly.

00:09:06.970 --> 00:09:09.980
That's why we created a
Cloud Composer to start with.

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:11.352
TOM MCGRATH: Definitely, right?

00:09:11.352 --> 00:09:13.310
That takes a little bit
of uplift, right, Feng?

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:14.120
FENG LU: Yeah.

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:14.420
TOM MCGRATH: Yeah.

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:14.920
[LAUGHING]

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:15.730
FENG LU: Totally.

00:09:15.730 --> 00:09:17.271
TOM MCGRATH: And
projects can contain

00:09:17.271 --> 00:09:18.380
more than one environment.

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:21.980
And so these integrate with
multiple different services--

00:09:21.980 --> 00:09:24.170
Cloud Storage to
manage your workflows,

00:09:24.170 --> 00:09:26.030
Stackdriver to understand
what's happening

00:09:26.030 --> 00:09:27.570
within the environment.

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:29.720
And then, of course, we're
securing the environment

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:34.430
with IAM, which we'll
explain in a moment.

00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:38.500
So functionality-wise,
these different methods

00:09:38.500 --> 00:09:41.517
of accessing Cloud
Composer-- all the same.

00:09:41.517 --> 00:09:43.600
One of the things that's
interesting to point out,

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:47.260
though, is with the
SDK, we've actually

00:09:47.260 --> 00:09:50.410
made it convenient to
manage Composer and Airflow

00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:53.180
both from the same
command line tool.

00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:56.440
So with G Cloud,
having that installed,

00:09:56.440 --> 00:09:58.032
I can then run my
Airflow commands

00:09:58.032 --> 00:09:58.990
against my environment.

00:09:58.990 --> 00:09:59.698
FENG LU: Exactly.

00:09:59.698 --> 00:10:00.520
Isn't that cool?

00:10:00.520 --> 00:10:01.790
TOM MCGRATH: That's awesome.

00:10:01.790 --> 00:10:02.800
That's awesome
that you guys were

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:03.940
able to pull that together.

00:10:03.940 --> 00:10:06.010
You have the tunnel
and the commands just

00:10:06.010 --> 00:10:06.970
flow right through.

00:10:06.970 --> 00:10:07.240
FENG LU: Right.

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:09.781
TOM MCGRATH: And you can manage
everything in the same place.

00:10:09.781 --> 00:10:12.020
Super easy.

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:13.750
So when we look at
the architecture,

00:10:13.750 --> 00:10:16.450
it's really a collection
of GCP resources.

00:10:16.450 --> 00:10:18.790
So you'll notice off the bat
that there's two projects.

00:10:18.790 --> 00:10:20.410
There's a customer
project and then

00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:22.660
there's the tenant project.

00:10:22.660 --> 00:10:24.220
The former is visible.

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:28.060
The customer project, you see
that running within your Cloud

00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:29.640
Console.

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:32.830
The tenant project is
actually managed by GCP.

00:10:32.830 --> 00:10:35.190
So that was a design
decision made because

00:10:35.190 --> 00:10:39.850
of Airflow's microservice feel.

00:10:39.850 --> 00:10:42.060
Do you want to comment on
that really quick, Feng?

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:42.810
FENG LU: Oh, yeah.

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:46.140
So there are Airflow
construct service

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:47.830
made up of a few components.

00:10:47.830 --> 00:10:50.130
They have a web server,
they have a scheduler,

00:10:50.130 --> 00:10:51.300
they have a worker.

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:53.970
So a different
microservice component

00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:57.930
needs to run [INAUDIBLE] phase
into different GCP services.

00:10:57.930 --> 00:11:00.150
For example, like
with web server,

00:11:00.150 --> 00:11:03.390
naturally fit into
the [? GA ?] domain.

00:11:03.390 --> 00:11:07.680
Likewise for scheduling
worker, sometimes those

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:08.680
need to communicate.

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:10.700
You need to have the right--

00:11:10.700 --> 00:11:13.470
it's good that we can fit
those into our GCP service that

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:15.720
provides communication
from [INAUDIBLE] app.

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:20.730
And Google Kubernetes
clusters will be a good place

00:11:20.730 --> 00:11:24.110
to host some of those
Airflow resources.

00:11:24.110 --> 00:11:25.110
TOM MCGRATH: Absolutely.

00:11:25.110 --> 00:11:27.052
And being able to scale up
and scale down is necessary.

00:11:27.052 --> 00:11:27.970
FENG LU: Exactly.

00:11:27.970 --> 00:11:28.470
Right.

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:29.220
TOM MCGRATH: One of
the other things that I

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:31.260
like about the design
too is that when

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:33.420
we look at the
Airflow database, that

00:11:33.420 --> 00:11:35.370
was obscured away
in a tenant project,

00:11:35.370 --> 00:11:36.660
so it could be secured down.

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:39.900
Because when we think about
connections to other systems,

00:11:39.900 --> 00:11:43.050
we don't want those
connection details to get out.

00:11:43.050 --> 00:11:45.360
We want those locked
down and secured away,

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:48.180
and by keeping it away
in the tenant project,

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:49.625
that's taken care of basically.

00:11:49.625 --> 00:11:50.250
FENG LU: Right.

00:11:50.250 --> 00:11:53.130
So here we really tried
to restrict the access

00:11:53.130 --> 00:11:56.520
to your Airflow
[INAUDIBLE] database,

00:11:56.520 --> 00:12:00.480
because it contains a lot
of connection credentials.

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:03.690
But at the same time, I
realize that sometimes you do

00:12:03.690 --> 00:12:06.510
need to get a database access.

00:12:06.510 --> 00:12:09.820
That is entirely possible by
downloading for the Kubernetes

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:12.859
engine that you use to run
your Airflow deployment.

00:12:12.859 --> 00:12:13.650
TOM MCGRATH: Right.

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:14.460
Exactly.

00:12:14.460 --> 00:12:16.667
And one other things I also
think that's really cool

00:12:16.667 --> 00:12:18.500
is that when you think
about the things that

00:12:18.500 --> 00:12:20.010
are inside the
tenant project, those

00:12:20.010 --> 00:12:21.840
are things that
don't necessarily

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:23.670
need to be touched, right?

00:12:23.670 --> 00:12:26.600
They can just operate and run,
and that's all well and good

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:27.850
from a customer's perspective.

00:12:27.850 --> 00:12:28.600
FENG LU: That's right.

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:28.830
Yeah.

00:12:28.830 --> 00:12:30.621
TOM MCGRATH: And the
other thing I like too

00:12:30.621 --> 00:12:34.950
is that in terms of locking down
the access to the Airflow web

00:12:34.950 --> 00:12:38.580
server, you leverage the
Identity-Aware Proxy,

00:12:38.580 --> 00:12:43.800
so the user can just sign in
using their IAM credentials

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:45.600
and gaining access that way.

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:48.870
They don't have to actually do
some sort of proxy tunneling

00:12:48.870 --> 00:12:50.000
or anything like that.

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:50.583
FENG LU: True.

00:12:50.583 --> 00:12:52.320
Yeah, that's the
reason why we decided

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:53.760
to put this in
the tenant project

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:57.780
and also put it behind the IAP,
so that it's always, always

00:12:57.780 --> 00:13:00.874
accessible to you without
all the proxying set up.

00:13:00.874 --> 00:13:02.790
One more thing I want
to mention is the reason

00:13:02.790 --> 00:13:04.495
why we put things
in tenant project

00:13:04.495 --> 00:13:09.240
is so that we can hide all the
IAP details from customers.

00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:13.050
You don't have to learn IAP in
order to use Cloud Composer.

00:13:13.050 --> 00:13:14.700
Everything is being
managed for you.

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:16.325
TOM MCGRATH: That's
an excellent point.

00:13:16.325 --> 00:13:17.010
Excellent point.

00:13:17.010 --> 00:13:20.730
And then of course, when it
comes to leveraging DAGs, which

00:13:20.730 --> 00:13:23.820
are basically your workflows
and logs and plug-ins,

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:25.662
that's all done
through Cloud Storage.

00:13:25.662 --> 00:13:27.870
And then of course, logging
goes into the Stackdriver

00:13:27.870 --> 00:13:28.766
and we'll see that.

00:13:28.766 --> 00:13:30.390
FENG LU: Deploying
a DAG is super easy.

00:13:30.390 --> 00:13:31.390
Just copy a file to GCS.

00:13:31.390 --> 00:13:32.650
That's all you need to do.

00:13:32.650 --> 00:13:35.700
TOM MCGRATH: Yeah, it's so
easy moving files to GCS,

00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:39.120
so no problems there.

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:40.980
So let's see what an
actual workflow looks

00:13:40.980 --> 00:13:42.930
like at a very high level.

00:13:42.930 --> 00:13:45.600
It's basically a collection of
tasks with interdependencies

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:47.140
and relationships.

00:13:47.140 --> 00:13:51.720
So basically, tasks
associate with workflows.

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:54.420
Dependencies wait until the
data is available in GCS,

00:13:54.420 --> 00:13:55.290
for example.

00:13:55.290 --> 00:13:56.790
And then I start
my BigQuery job.

00:13:56.790 --> 00:13:58.770
Or it triggers something, right?

00:13:58.770 --> 00:14:01.930
That file is actually
available within HDFS,

00:14:01.930 --> 00:14:04.800
and let's copy it
into GCS from there.

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:07.860
So these are really the building
blocks of workflows in Airflow.

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:10.740
And then of course, maybe
when my job completes,

00:14:10.740 --> 00:14:12.720
then I want to notify
my users on Slack

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:15.930
so they know that the most
up-to-date data and information

00:14:15.930 --> 00:14:17.015
is available to them.

00:14:17.015 --> 00:14:17.598
FENG LU: Yeah.

00:14:19.940 --> 00:14:23.000
TOM MCGRATH: So Airflow
DAGs are effectively

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:25.190
Python files with
five main sections,

00:14:25.190 --> 00:14:29.130
and we'll cover each
of those in turn.

00:14:29.130 --> 00:14:30.570
So import, right?

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:32.010
Step one, import.

00:14:32.010 --> 00:14:37.170
From the Airflow system, a DAG
or a Directed Acyclic Graph

00:14:37.170 --> 00:14:40.150
is basically another
way of saying workflow.

00:14:40.150 --> 00:14:43.290
So we import the operators
to be used in the workflow.

00:14:43.290 --> 00:14:45.210
For example, the
BigQuery operator

00:14:45.210 --> 00:14:48.090
you can see there
on the fourth line.

00:14:48.090 --> 00:14:51.060
And then from there, we can
trigger rules to specify

00:14:51.060 --> 00:14:52.770
the intertask relationships.

00:14:55.410 --> 00:14:57.630
Then the arguments are defined.

00:14:57.630 --> 00:15:00.880
So we might have some
default DAG arguments,

00:15:00.880 --> 00:15:03.074
and that's a convenient
thing for Airflow.

00:15:03.074 --> 00:15:04.740
But if there's common
arguments that you

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:07.620
want to leverage, instead
of specifying those

00:15:07.620 --> 00:15:10.470
for each Airflow
task, you can actually

00:15:10.470 --> 00:15:13.620
specify those at the
beginning of your workflow,

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:15.600
and then pass them
in automatically

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:17.430
via the Airflow DAG model.

00:15:17.430 --> 00:15:18.677
I think that's super cool.

00:15:18.677 --> 00:15:19.260
FENG LU: Yeah.

00:15:19.260 --> 00:15:20.010
TOM MCGRATH: Yeah.

00:15:20.010 --> 00:15:23.299
FENG LU: Saves a lot of boiler
plate coding in your workflow.

00:15:23.299 --> 00:15:25.590
TOM MCGRATH: Yeah, absolutely,
and lots of flexibility.

00:15:25.590 --> 00:15:26.173
FENG LU: Yeah.

00:15:26.173 --> 00:15:28.070
TOM MCGRATH: Yeah.

00:15:28.070 --> 00:15:31.650
So in terms of setup, you
basically give it a name--

00:15:31.650 --> 00:15:33.830
so in this case, demo_dag--

00:15:33.830 --> 00:15:36.980
a schedule interview
and interval, and also

00:15:36.980 --> 00:15:38.630
default arguments.

00:15:38.630 --> 00:15:41.060
Very simple, straightforward,
easy to understand.

00:15:43.670 --> 00:15:45.550
And then we define the tasks.

00:15:45.550 --> 00:15:50.170
So here you can see that we have
a BigQuery operator and also

00:15:50.170 --> 00:15:52.230
a BigQuery to GCS operator.

00:15:52.230 --> 00:15:54.160
So within the BigQuery
operator, we're

00:15:54.160 --> 00:15:57.130
running a BigQuery query
that will export the results

00:15:57.130 --> 00:15:58.910
to a new data set,
and you can see

00:15:58.910 --> 00:16:02.620
where it's specifying the name
of the table with BigQuery.

00:16:02.620 --> 00:16:05.290
But then we also have the
BigQuery to Cloud Storage

00:16:05.290 --> 00:16:07.960
operator, which is
exporting that new data set

00:16:07.960 --> 00:16:09.740
to Cloud Storage.

00:16:09.740 --> 00:16:12.190
Once again, easy integration.

00:16:12.190 --> 00:16:14.317
Very simple and
easy to understand.

00:16:14.317 --> 00:16:16.150
FENG LU: Plus, the code
is not hard to read.

00:16:16.150 --> 00:16:17.260
TOM MCGRATH: Oh, no.

00:16:17.260 --> 00:16:18.050
Absolutely not.

00:16:18.050 --> 00:16:21.260
Yeah, this is simple and
straightforward stuff.

00:16:21.260 --> 00:16:22.570
That's the purpose.

00:16:22.570 --> 00:16:23.190
FENG LU: Yeah.

00:16:23.190 --> 00:16:25.380
TOM MCGRATH: Yeah.

00:16:25.380 --> 00:16:28.930
So in terms of
ordering, the tasks

00:16:28.930 --> 00:16:31.600
can be ordered based on
completion dependencies.

00:16:31.600 --> 00:16:34.250
One thing must finish
before the other.

00:16:34.250 --> 00:16:37.120
For example, my
Airflow_commits_query,

00:16:37.120 --> 00:16:39.460
the query that runs that
creates my data set,

00:16:39.460 --> 00:16:44.400
must finish before I want
to export that data to GCS.

00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:45.420
Makes sense.

00:16:45.420 --> 00:16:46.990
It's that easy.

00:16:46.990 --> 00:16:49.960
So at this point we're
going to switch to the demo.

00:16:49.960 --> 00:16:54.130
Feng is going to
present that for us.

00:16:54.130 --> 00:16:55.176
So take it away, Feng.

00:16:55.176 --> 00:16:56.050
FENG LU: Thanks, Tom.

00:16:56.050 --> 00:16:58.559
So so far we have been talking
about all the nice things

00:16:58.559 --> 00:16:59.600
about the Cloud Composer.

00:16:59.600 --> 00:17:02.800
Now let's see how we put
everything in action.

00:17:02.800 --> 00:17:06.550
We mentioned that to get things
started, the first thing you

00:17:06.550 --> 00:17:09.760
probably want to do is go to--
there a few places we mentioned

00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:11.560
about Cloud Console.

00:17:11.560 --> 00:17:13.869
We mentioned about a Cloud SDK.

00:17:13.869 --> 00:17:16.359
Here we'll just
use Cloud Console

00:17:16.359 --> 00:17:19.725
as a way to demonstrate how
to play with Cloud Composer.

00:17:19.725 --> 00:17:24.400
A similar functionality can
be achieved using Cloud SDK.

00:17:24.400 --> 00:17:27.640
Most of the cases, what
you want to do is just go--

00:17:27.640 --> 00:17:32.200
once you are in the Cloud
Composer page in Cloud Console,

00:17:32.200 --> 00:17:36.310
say you want to create
a composing environment.

00:17:36.310 --> 00:17:39.600
Click the Create and that
leads you to the Create page.

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:41.320
Here you can specify your name.

00:17:41.320 --> 00:17:43.510
Say, hey, demo.

00:17:43.510 --> 00:17:46.510
And then you can specify all the
arguments or all the properties

00:17:46.510 --> 00:17:47.819
of that environment.

00:17:47.819 --> 00:17:50.110
I'm going to walk you through
some of the configuration

00:17:50.110 --> 00:17:52.310
options that you might have.

00:17:52.310 --> 00:17:54.340
You can always
specify the region

00:17:54.340 --> 00:17:58.600
where you want to create
your composing environment.

00:17:58.600 --> 00:18:02.140
You can also specify things
like a machine type, number

00:18:02.140 --> 00:18:06.810
of nodes, all scope, and also
I want to highlight one thing.

00:18:06.810 --> 00:18:08.415
For better isolation
and protection,

00:18:08.415 --> 00:18:11.260
we will also allow you to
specify a service account you

00:18:11.260 --> 00:18:13.060
use to run the
composing environment,

00:18:13.060 --> 00:18:17.044
so you don't have to use the
generic default Compute Engine

00:18:17.044 --> 00:18:17.710
service account.

00:18:17.710 --> 00:18:20.402
You can use any service
account at your choice.

00:18:20.402 --> 00:18:21.360
TOM MCGRATH: Very cool.

00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:21.943
FENG LU: Yeah.

00:18:21.943 --> 00:18:23.280
Scroll down a little bit.

00:18:23.280 --> 00:18:26.350
That section is about
some Airflow configuration

00:18:26.350 --> 00:18:27.100
properties.

00:18:27.100 --> 00:18:30.200
And then Airflow itself
comes with a long list

00:18:30.200 --> 00:18:31.282
of configurations.

00:18:31.282 --> 00:18:32.740
TOM MCGRATH: How
many would you say

00:18:32.740 --> 00:18:33.615
there are, if you had to guess?

00:18:33.615 --> 00:18:36.550
FENG LU: I think it's probably
100-plus configuration options,

00:18:36.550 --> 00:18:38.500
but you are not going
to use all of those.

00:18:38.500 --> 00:18:38.830
TOM MCGRATH: Good point.

00:18:38.830 --> 00:18:40.690
FENG LU: Say some
common ones, I would

00:18:40.690 --> 00:18:43.300
say, if you want to
decide how soon you

00:18:43.300 --> 00:18:44.520
want to reload your DAG file.

00:18:44.520 --> 00:18:45.978
That's probably
something you care,

00:18:45.978 --> 00:18:48.030
because every time
you make a change,

00:18:48.030 --> 00:18:50.050
you want Airflow to pick
up that change quickly,

00:18:50.050 --> 00:18:51.935
so that's something
you can config.

00:18:51.935 --> 00:18:55.420
And moving down, we also
have environment variables.

00:18:55.420 --> 00:18:58.980
Those are the ones if you want
to send an email right now,

00:18:58.980 --> 00:19:01.944
a composer comes by together
with SendGrid email support.

00:19:01.944 --> 00:19:02.860
TOM MCGRATH: Oh, nice.

00:19:02.860 --> 00:19:05.026
FENG LU: So you would config
a composing environment

00:19:05.026 --> 00:19:07.570
by just adding two
environment variables,

00:19:07.570 --> 00:19:09.526
like SendGrid mail from
and SendGrid API key.

00:19:09.526 --> 00:19:10.567
TOM MCGRATH: Very simple.

00:19:10.567 --> 00:19:13.490
FENG LU: Those will get you
set up with email capabilities.

00:19:13.490 --> 00:19:14.920
TOM MCGRATH: Excellent.

00:19:14.920 --> 00:19:19.335
FENG LU: Labels-- if you want to
basically conveniently navigate

00:19:19.335 --> 00:19:22.380
through composing environments
and even across other GC

00:19:22.380 --> 00:19:25.810
resources, you can always use
labels attached to environments

00:19:25.810 --> 00:19:27.340
to [INAUDIBLE] on
those labels, you

00:19:27.340 --> 00:19:29.080
would be able to get
your environment.

00:19:29.080 --> 00:19:31.930
I'm probably going to skip the
part of creating a composing

00:19:31.930 --> 00:19:32.800
environment.

00:19:32.800 --> 00:19:37.300
Reason because it may take
10-plus minutes, not because--

00:19:37.300 --> 00:19:39.390
the reason is we need
to create an App Engine,

00:19:39.390 --> 00:19:42.150
and it takes a while for
the App Engine to get ready.

00:19:42.150 --> 00:19:45.304
So let's assume that you already
have the environment ready.

00:19:45.304 --> 00:19:45.970
TOM MCGRATH: OK.

00:19:45.970 --> 00:19:46.656
FENG LU: Yeah.

00:19:46.656 --> 00:19:48.312
TOM MCGRATH: Perfect.

00:19:48.312 --> 00:19:49.395
FENG LU: So let's go back.

00:19:52.480 --> 00:19:56.336
So we have a composer demo
environment created beforehand.

00:19:56.336 --> 00:19:58.540
And once you have the
environment ready,

00:19:58.540 --> 00:20:00.680
you can go in and inspect
the various properties

00:20:00.680 --> 00:20:02.590
of that environment.

00:20:02.590 --> 00:20:05.080
Here you get the overview,
like how many number

00:20:05.080 --> 00:20:08.860
of nodes, service accounts,
[INAUDIBLE] scope,

00:20:08.860 --> 00:20:12.230
and you also get to see that
two important things here.

00:20:12.230 --> 00:20:14.800
One is the host of
the Airflow web UI.

00:20:14.800 --> 00:20:17.360
The second thing is
the G-SaaS packet.

00:20:17.360 --> 00:20:21.280
The Airflow web UI allows
you to inspect workflows.

00:20:21.280 --> 00:20:23.110
And then the G-SaaS
packet is really

00:20:23.110 --> 00:20:26.889
for you to upload and make
changes to your DAG files.

00:20:26.889 --> 00:20:27.930
TOM MCGRATH: Makes sense.

00:20:27.930 --> 00:20:29.305
FENG LU: So now
let's go and take

00:20:29.305 --> 00:20:30.860
a look at a hosted web server.

00:20:30.860 --> 00:20:32.610
TOM MCGRATH: Yeah,
let's see it in action.

00:20:32.610 --> 00:20:34.980
FENG LU: Yeah.

00:20:34.980 --> 00:20:39.450
So as I mentioned
earlier, the web server

00:20:39.450 --> 00:20:42.480
is hosted in GIE,
protected by IAP.

00:20:42.480 --> 00:20:44.610
If you first login
to this page, it

00:20:44.610 --> 00:20:48.750
will ask you for your
Google credentials to login.

00:20:48.750 --> 00:20:53.250
It can be your Gmail
account, or you can also

00:20:53.250 --> 00:20:55.740
have [INAUDIBLE] access
through a web server

00:20:55.740 --> 00:20:56.880
by using a service account.

00:20:56.880 --> 00:20:58.680
Both ways are possible.

00:20:58.680 --> 00:21:02.640
And then once you are
in the Airflow web UI,

00:21:02.640 --> 00:21:05.130
you could start to inspect
the various workflows

00:21:05.130 --> 00:21:06.870
you have submitted.

00:21:06.870 --> 00:21:12.700
For example, earlier we have Tom
walk you through the BigQ demo.

00:21:12.700 --> 00:21:15.750
So we can inspect and check
a few details of that.

00:21:18.260 --> 00:21:19.520
You have a few tasks.

00:21:19.520 --> 00:21:21.620
Those tasks are
chained in some order.

00:21:21.620 --> 00:21:22.800
Not very fancy.

00:21:22.800 --> 00:21:27.020
I understand it's really
like a linear chain of tasks,

00:21:27.020 --> 00:21:29.360
but you can create it
as complicated workflows

00:21:29.360 --> 00:21:30.050
as you want.

00:21:30.050 --> 00:21:31.550
So for the time
now, this has really

00:21:31.550 --> 00:21:35.090
tried to connect you back to
the example you have just seen,

00:21:35.090 --> 00:21:37.010
right?

00:21:37.010 --> 00:21:39.590
You create a data site,
you run BigQ query,

00:21:39.590 --> 00:21:41.360
and then you export
the data to G-SaaS.

00:21:41.360 --> 00:21:42.110
TOM MCGRATH: Yeah.

00:21:42.110 --> 00:21:43.760
I've definitely
seen customers who

00:21:43.760 --> 00:21:49.040
are running Airflow where their
diagrams or their graph views

00:21:49.040 --> 00:21:50.542
easily take up the whole screen.

00:21:50.542 --> 00:21:51.500
FENG LU: Exactly, yeah.

00:21:51.500 --> 00:21:53.708
TOM MCGRATH: So this is, of
course, a very simplistic

00:21:53.708 --> 00:21:55.760
example, but it shows
the idea of what's

00:21:55.760 --> 00:21:57.014
possible within Airflow.

00:21:57.014 --> 00:21:57.930
FENG LU: Right, right.

00:21:57.930 --> 00:21:58.250
TOM MCGRATH: Yeah.

00:21:58.250 --> 00:22:00.210
FENG LU: And then
there are various views

00:22:00.210 --> 00:22:02.750
of that DAG workflow.

00:22:02.750 --> 00:22:06.600
It has the graph view,
you've got a tree view,

00:22:06.600 --> 00:22:08.120
and then personally
I myself also

00:22:08.120 --> 00:22:12.460
find the [INAUDIBLE]
there's a Gantt chart view.

00:22:12.460 --> 00:22:12.960
I'm sorry.

00:22:12.960 --> 00:22:14.440
This one shows the timeline.

00:22:17.650 --> 00:22:19.260
This is the Gantt chart view.

00:22:19.260 --> 00:22:21.210
This allows you to
put all your task

00:22:21.210 --> 00:22:23.490
completion in a single place.

00:22:23.490 --> 00:22:26.090
You would be able to say,
hey, when a task gets started

00:22:26.090 --> 00:22:26.980
or when--

00:22:26.980 --> 00:22:29.366
where you spend most
time on a specific task.

00:22:29.366 --> 00:22:30.990
TOM MCGRATH: So this
is really helpful,

00:22:30.990 --> 00:22:35.550
because then we can basically
look at everything in one place

00:22:35.550 --> 00:22:37.500
and understand where
the bottlenecks are.

00:22:37.500 --> 00:22:38.458
FENG LU: Exactly, yeah.

00:22:38.458 --> 00:22:41.730
Then you can decide from that
point, how should you optimize

00:22:41.730 --> 00:22:42.810
your workflow pipeline?

00:22:42.810 --> 00:22:43.643
TOM MCGRATH: Got it.

00:22:43.643 --> 00:22:44.780
FENG LU: Yeah.

00:22:44.780 --> 00:22:47.121
Let's go back and then
go to the homepage.

00:22:47.121 --> 00:22:49.329
[INAUDIBLE] that's actually
you start a workflow run.

00:22:53.730 --> 00:22:57.430
Here you can easily
trigger a workflow run

00:22:57.430 --> 00:23:00.105
from the Airflow web page.

00:23:00.105 --> 00:23:03.450
Once a workflow run is created,
you save the workflow runs.

00:23:03.450 --> 00:23:06.060
The status that you have
a one run says running,

00:23:06.060 --> 00:23:08.244
and then unfortunately
you probably

00:23:08.244 --> 00:23:09.660
have to refresh
the page from time

00:23:09.660 --> 00:23:12.300
to time so that you
can see a task being

00:23:12.300 --> 00:23:13.607
moved from various stages.

00:23:13.607 --> 00:23:14.440
TOM MCGRATH: Got it.

00:23:14.440 --> 00:23:17.250
FENG LU: So here we have seen
that we have a task that's

00:23:17.250 --> 00:23:19.400
right now in the running state.

00:23:19.400 --> 00:23:25.070
It's probably done processing,
but you can go back to inspect.

00:23:25.070 --> 00:23:27.390
There's another one.

00:23:27.390 --> 00:23:33.120
If you go to the graph
view, you can see

00:23:33.120 --> 00:23:36.570
the various data of the task.

00:23:36.570 --> 00:23:39.340
And sometimes if you're
curious to understand

00:23:39.340 --> 00:23:41.940
the logs that's
produced by the task,

00:23:41.940 --> 00:23:43.800
you can always go inspect here.

00:23:43.800 --> 00:23:45.140
TOM MCGRATH: And that's
a common request, too.

00:23:45.140 --> 00:23:45.900
FENG LU: Customer request, yes.

00:23:45.900 --> 00:23:48.358
TOM MCGRATH: A lot of users
want to be able to see the logs

00:23:48.358 --> 00:23:50.420
and see what's going on,
especially from Airflow

00:23:50.420 --> 00:23:52.560
when it's touching so
many different systems.

00:23:52.560 --> 00:23:55.575
FENG LU: Exactly, and
then often sometimes

00:23:55.575 --> 00:23:58.230
when you have task
failures, that's probably

00:23:58.230 --> 00:24:00.180
when logs become most useful.

00:24:00.180 --> 00:24:04.940
So we understand sometimes a
failure may come out from--

00:24:04.940 --> 00:24:07.125
a fault may not necessarily
happen within Airflow.

00:24:07.125 --> 00:24:07.750
Think about it.

00:24:07.750 --> 00:24:09.850
You have a process crash.

00:24:09.850 --> 00:24:12.510
You have a container restart.

00:24:12.510 --> 00:24:16.260
Those events are not
uncommon in Kubernetes.

00:24:16.260 --> 00:24:20.370
We provide Stackdriver logs
so that your logs are always

00:24:20.370 --> 00:24:22.440
kept somewhere, even
for the logs that

00:24:22.440 --> 00:24:24.412
happen outside Airflow.

00:24:24.412 --> 00:24:26.220
TOM MCGRATH: Very cool.

00:24:26.220 --> 00:24:28.260
Very helpful for debugging
and seeing what's

00:24:28.260 --> 00:24:29.245
actually going on.

00:24:29.245 --> 00:24:29.870
FENG LU: Right.

00:24:29.870 --> 00:24:32.530
Yeah, so if you go to our
Stackdriver login page,

00:24:32.530 --> 00:24:36.970
we actually have a dedicated
Cloud Composer stop there.

00:24:36.970 --> 00:24:39.510
You can navigate there,
try to figure out which

00:24:39.510 --> 00:24:42.030
environment, which
region, and which

00:24:42.030 --> 00:24:46.264
environment you have your
Airflow environment created.

00:24:46.264 --> 00:24:47.680
You can select
those environments,

00:24:47.680 --> 00:24:48.960
and then once you have
selected your environment,

00:24:48.960 --> 00:24:50.918
you will be able to see
all the logs pertaining

00:24:50.918 --> 00:24:53.340
to your environments.

00:24:53.340 --> 00:24:58.530
We tried to pre-classify those
logs into a few categories.

00:24:58.530 --> 00:25:01.740
We have Airflow schedule
logs, worker logs.

00:25:01.740 --> 00:25:05.190
That helps you to
better manage your logs.

00:25:05.190 --> 00:25:08.640
One more thing we have added
to the Airflow worker logs

00:25:08.640 --> 00:25:13.220
is we do allow you to
filter logs by DAG ID, task

00:25:13.220 --> 00:25:14.580
ID, and execution date.

00:25:14.580 --> 00:25:14.820
TOM MCGRATH: Ah.

00:25:14.820 --> 00:25:16.194
FENG LU: It will
quickly help you

00:25:16.194 --> 00:25:18.361
to navigate to the specific
task you're looking for.

00:25:18.361 --> 00:25:19.860
TOM MCGRATH: And
then you can really

00:25:19.860 --> 00:25:21.750
narrow your way through
the logs as opposed

00:25:21.750 --> 00:25:22.830
to looking at everything.

00:25:22.830 --> 00:25:23.455
FENG LU: Right.

00:25:23.455 --> 00:25:25.960
Otherwise you're just seeing
this continuous stream of logs,

00:25:25.960 --> 00:25:26.980
and you off--

00:25:26.980 --> 00:25:30.054
worse that those logs you may
mix were from concurrent tasks.

00:25:30.054 --> 00:25:31.470
TOM MCGRATH: Of
course, of course.

00:25:31.470 --> 00:25:34.580
Because chances are you probably
have multiple different DAGs,

00:25:34.580 --> 00:25:36.895
perhaps even sub-DAGs,
which we'll get into.

00:25:36.895 --> 00:25:37.740
FENG LU: Absolutely.

00:25:37.740 --> 00:25:39.690
TOM MCGRATH: It can easily
become quite complicated.

00:25:39.690 --> 00:25:39.945
FENG LU: Exactly.

00:25:39.945 --> 00:25:40.200
Yeah.

00:25:40.200 --> 00:25:42.440
TOM MCGRATH: But this removes
all that complication.

00:25:42.440 --> 00:25:42.760
FENG LU: Yeah.

00:25:42.760 --> 00:25:43.510
TOM MCGRATH: Yeah.

00:25:43.510 --> 00:25:45.690
FENG LU: That's how
we saw that-- how

00:25:45.690 --> 00:25:47.940
we could Stackdriver
logging useful for you

00:25:47.940 --> 00:25:49.525
for debugging Airflow DAGs.

00:25:49.525 --> 00:25:50.400
TOM MCGRATH: Awesome.

00:25:50.400 --> 00:25:50.983
FENG LU: Yeah.

