00:29:14.080 --> 00:29:16.700
TOM MCGRATH: That's OK.

00:29:16.700 --> 00:29:18.650
FENG LU: So the task is running.

00:29:18.650 --> 00:29:21.492
We can inspect the log.

00:29:21.492 --> 00:29:23.450
That's one more thing I
want to highlight here,

00:29:23.450 --> 00:29:25.270
which is perfect
from this example.

00:29:25.270 --> 00:29:28.080
Sometimes, there might be
some delay from Airflow

00:29:28.080 --> 00:29:32.830
when they try to collect logs
and display logs in a web UI.

00:29:32.830 --> 00:29:34.870
Because you try to
do things in pairs

00:29:34.870 --> 00:29:36.830
to save a lot of
read-write cost.

00:29:36.830 --> 00:29:39.129
But then with Stackdriver,
you got logs in real time,

00:29:39.129 --> 00:29:41.545
so you don't have to wait--
always in [? tears. ?] Imagine

00:29:41.545 --> 00:29:46.065
that you have, like a task that
may take hours to complete,

00:29:46.065 --> 00:29:47.440
and in the middle,
you don't want

00:29:47.440 --> 00:29:49.060
to just wait hours-- be
able to see the logs.

00:29:49.060 --> 00:29:49.290
TOM MCGRATH: I know.

00:29:49.290 --> 00:29:50.790
You want to see the
logs right away.

00:29:50.790 --> 00:29:52.166
You want to know
what's going on.

00:29:52.166 --> 00:29:52.748
FENG LU: Yeah.

00:29:52.748 --> 00:29:54.850
That's how stack driver
could come in and help,

00:29:54.850 --> 00:29:56.980
so that you can inspect
logs in real time.

00:29:56.980 --> 00:29:57.430
TOM MCGRATH: Awesome.

00:29:57.430 --> 00:29:57.971
FENG LU: Yep.

00:30:01.070 --> 00:30:03.845
Again, let's just give it
some time for it to finish.

00:30:07.670 --> 00:30:12.154
Well, while we're waiting
for the demo to complete,

00:30:12.154 --> 00:30:13.820
the other thing I
also want to mention--

00:30:13.820 --> 00:30:15.740
things are very
useful on the Airflow

00:30:15.740 --> 00:30:22.750
web UI is it also gives a place
for you to retrigger DAGs.

00:30:22.750 --> 00:30:26.077
But also a common
issue people run into

00:30:26.077 --> 00:30:28.160
is like, what if your DAG
runs into an error state

00:30:28.160 --> 00:30:30.380
and you want to recover
from that error state?

00:30:30.380 --> 00:30:34.104
Things you can do here is--

00:30:34.104 --> 00:30:36.530
it's something manual, but
it's still better for you

00:30:36.530 --> 00:30:39.170
to rerun the entire pipeline.

00:30:39.170 --> 00:30:43.970
What do you can do is by delete
or clear the failed task.

00:30:43.970 --> 00:30:46.640
And after that, you set
your workflow status

00:30:46.640 --> 00:30:48.260
from failed to running.

00:30:48.260 --> 00:30:51.180
And then after the workflow
status changed to running,

00:30:51.180 --> 00:30:53.650
Airflow scheduler will
be able to pick up

00:30:53.650 --> 00:30:56.010
on and then reschedule
the previous failed task.

00:30:56.010 --> 00:30:57.343
TOM MCGRATH: Oh, that's awesome.

00:30:57.343 --> 00:31:00.800
So you can pick up right from
exactly where the DAG left off

00:31:00.800 --> 00:31:01.707
effectively.

00:31:01.707 --> 00:31:02.290
FENG LU: Yeah.

00:31:02.290 --> 00:31:02.930
TOM MCGRATH: Nice.

00:31:02.930 --> 00:31:05.055
FENG LU: We're also trying
to see how we can better

00:31:05.055 --> 00:31:06.860
automate this
instead of you always

00:31:06.860 --> 00:31:09.969
have to perform this
multi-step action yourself.

00:31:09.969 --> 00:31:12.260
But at least there's a way
for you to do that right now

00:31:12.260 --> 00:31:12.830
in Airflow.

00:31:12.830 --> 00:31:14.740
TOM MCGRATH: Absolutely.

