00:32:34.400 --> 00:32:37.920
I only need to touch this single
parameter with each invocation

00:32:37.920 --> 00:32:39.200
of my diagram.

00:32:39.200 --> 00:32:43.040
So that's what's supporting
Airflow was templating.

00:32:43.040 --> 00:32:44.540
Just put whatever
parameter you need

00:32:44.540 --> 00:32:48.440
to templarise as an
encapsulating [INAUDIBLE]

00:32:48.440 --> 00:32:49.322
template.

00:32:49.322 --> 00:32:50.780
And then you can
always instantiate

00:32:50.780 --> 00:32:51.825
that as a DAG runtime.

00:32:51.825 --> 00:32:52.700
TOM MCGRATH: Awesome.

00:32:52.700 --> 00:32:53.283
FENG LU: Yeah.

00:32:55.976 --> 00:32:59.950
Likewise, if your
DAG is complicated--

00:32:59.950 --> 00:33:01.677
think about if you have 500--

00:33:01.677 --> 00:33:03.760
you mentioned that you
have seen some DAG examples

00:33:03.760 --> 00:33:05.874
with like a few hundred tasks.

00:33:05.874 --> 00:33:06.790
TOM MCGRATH: At least.

00:33:06.790 --> 00:33:07.289
[LAUGHING]

00:33:07.289 --> 00:33:09.900
FENG LU: I realize it is so
tedious and probably also error

00:33:09.900 --> 00:33:13.450
prone for you to specify
those DAGs or tasks one by one

00:33:13.450 --> 00:33:13.990
yourself.

00:33:13.990 --> 00:33:14.960
TOM MCGRATH: Yeah, definitely.

00:33:14.960 --> 00:33:15.730
FENG LU: So here's another way.

00:33:15.730 --> 00:33:17.355
You can use programmatic
DAG generation

00:33:17.355 --> 00:33:19.890
where everything's been
done in your program.

00:33:19.890 --> 00:33:22.600
And that's also one of
the motivations behind why

00:33:22.600 --> 00:33:25.060
we want to use programming
languages to define and then

00:33:25.060 --> 00:33:25.610
create DAGs.

00:33:25.610 --> 00:33:26.860
TOM MCGRATH: Yeah, definitely.

00:33:26.860 --> 00:33:30.940
I mean, if I had to run
1,000 tasks of the same type,

00:33:30.940 --> 00:33:32.480
that would just be so tedious.

00:33:32.480 --> 00:33:35.295
And you know, with few lines
of code, we're able to do that.

00:33:35.295 --> 00:33:35.920
FENG LU: Right.

00:33:38.800 --> 00:33:43.210
And then the last thing is
when your DAGs has grown big,

00:33:43.210 --> 00:33:44.830
you want modularity.

00:33:44.830 --> 00:33:48.271
You want to be able to
create sub-components.

00:33:48.271 --> 00:33:51.460
Airflow has this concept
called subDAG that conveniently

00:33:51.460 --> 00:33:55.970
allows you to package a few
related tasks into a single sub

00:33:55.970 --> 00:33:56.470
DAG.

00:33:56.470 --> 00:33:58.480
And no single sub
DAG will be treated

00:33:58.480 --> 00:34:01.967
as if they're a single
task in the master DAG.

00:34:01.967 --> 00:34:03.800
TOM MCGRATH: That's
awesome for reusability.

00:34:03.800 --> 00:34:05.383
FENG LU: Reusability
and it also helps

00:34:05.383 --> 00:34:09.449
you to organize your tasks
in a hierarchical manner.

00:34:09.449 --> 00:34:11.527
TOM MCGRATH: Yeah.

00:34:11.527 --> 00:34:12.110
FENG LU: Cool.

00:34:12.110 --> 00:34:13.760
So those are all
the nice things you

00:34:13.760 --> 00:34:18.170
could do using Airflow, using
Composer, using its programming

00:34:18.170 --> 00:34:22.969
model to define the workflows.

00:34:22.969 --> 00:34:28.159
Some of the common questions we
have seen from our customers,

00:34:28.159 --> 00:34:29.810
can I install Python packages?

00:34:29.810 --> 00:34:31.020
We have just discussed that.

00:34:31.020 --> 00:34:34.070
In fact, often
more than that, you

00:34:34.070 --> 00:34:36.679
can bring any containers
into a composer

00:34:36.679 --> 00:34:38.596
where you can capture
your dependencies there.

00:34:38.596 --> 00:34:40.512
TOM MCGRATH: And something
that's interesting,

00:34:40.512 --> 00:34:43.310
right, is when you do that, when
you place your custom packages

00:34:43.310 --> 00:34:45.810
and custom code inside
that DAG folder,

00:34:45.810 --> 00:34:47.989
we actually recognize that
it's not actually a DAG.

00:34:47.989 --> 00:34:51.050
It doesn't have those
beginning DAG statements

00:34:51.050 --> 00:34:55.100
that we talked about earlier,
so airflow doesn't pick it up

00:34:55.100 --> 00:34:55.820
as a DAG.

00:34:55.820 --> 00:34:57.020
FENG LU: Exactly.

00:34:57.020 --> 00:34:59.570
TOM MCGRATH: That's cool.

00:34:59.570 --> 00:35:03.320
FENG LU: Environment will be
auto-upgraded on our roadmap.

00:35:03.320 --> 00:35:05.330
Not supported right
now, but that's

00:35:05.330 --> 00:35:08.030
something we're
working very hard on.

00:35:08.030 --> 00:35:10.490
Will our Composer be
offered in more regions?

00:35:10.490 --> 00:35:11.960
Absolutely, yes.

00:35:11.960 --> 00:35:13.730
That's our roadmap
to expand Composer

00:35:13.730 --> 00:35:16.189
to possibly one or two more
regions by the end of the year.

00:35:16.189 --> 00:35:18.438
TOM MCGRATH: And I think GA
is a good example of that,

00:35:18.438 --> 00:35:20.200
right, because with
the GA release,

00:35:20.200 --> 00:35:22.360
it was then available
in two more regions.

00:35:22.360 --> 00:35:24.500
FENG LU: Right.

00:35:24.500 --> 00:35:27.260
Is there a graphic
way to create DAGs?

00:35:27.260 --> 00:35:28.150
We hear you.

00:35:28.150 --> 00:35:28.650
Yes.

00:35:28.650 --> 00:35:29.045
[LAUGHING]

00:35:29.045 --> 00:35:29.440
Absolutely.

00:35:29.440 --> 00:35:30.450
TOM MCGRATH: Work in progress.

00:35:30.450 --> 00:35:32.130
FENG LU: Yeah, working
on-- in progress.

00:35:32.130 --> 00:35:34.880
We also want to make sure that
for the people who are not

00:35:34.880 --> 00:35:36.500
very comfortable
with programming--

00:35:36.500 --> 00:35:39.110
although we mentioned that
Airflow DAG model is really

00:35:39.110 --> 00:35:43.820
simple, but still, if you
prefer to use GUI for creating

00:35:43.820 --> 00:35:47.810
your DAGs, that's something
we see a value in,

00:35:47.810 --> 00:35:48.740
and we're working on.

00:35:48.740 --> 00:35:50.706
TOM MCGRATH: Definitely.

00:35:50.706 --> 00:35:52.580
FENG LU: Can I choose
which version of Python

00:35:52.580 --> 00:35:54.980
is using Composer?

00:35:54.980 --> 00:35:57.664
Yes, the support is
going to be out soon.

00:35:57.664 --> 00:35:59.330
Well, you could specify
whether you want

00:35:59.330 --> 00:36:00.789
to use Python 2 or Python 3.

00:36:00.789 --> 00:36:02.330
TOM MCGRATH: Stay
tuned for that one.

00:36:02.330 --> 00:36:03.038
FENG LU: Exactly.

00:36:06.650 --> 00:36:10.340
Sort of a broader scheme or
grand scheme of Composer,

00:36:10.340 --> 00:36:12.260
where we are heading
to, definitely

00:36:12.260 --> 00:36:14.480
want to see more close
Kubernetes and Airflow

00:36:14.480 --> 00:36:16.250
integrations.

00:36:16.250 --> 00:36:18.980
As I mentioned, I want to
save something at the end.

00:36:18.980 --> 00:36:19.874
So here we can use--

00:36:19.874 --> 00:36:21.040
TOM MCGRATH: Oh, here we go.

00:36:21.040 --> 00:36:22.630
FENG LU: Yeah.

00:36:22.630 --> 00:36:24.380
Soon, we're going to
make another release.

00:36:24.380 --> 00:36:26.090
In another release,
we're going to allow

00:36:26.090 --> 00:36:31.460
you to create GKE clusters--
deleted GK clusters.

00:36:31.460 --> 00:36:34.460
And also, more importantly,
you can launch a container

00:36:34.460 --> 00:36:36.939
in any GK cluster as long
as you have credentials.

00:36:36.939 --> 00:36:38.105
TOM MCGRATH: That's awesome.

00:36:38.105 --> 00:36:39.920
FENG LU: So think about that.

00:36:39.920 --> 00:36:43.430
We know, like GK just
promoted its GKE On-Prem,

00:36:43.430 --> 00:36:47.390
where if you could orchestrate
in a launcher container

00:36:47.390 --> 00:36:51.190
in a remote cluster that
is hosted in your On-Prem,

00:36:51.190 --> 00:36:53.480
eventually, that gives you
a convenient way for you

00:36:53.480 --> 00:36:55.895
to build hybrid
orchestration in GCP.

00:36:55.895 --> 00:36:57.770
TOM MCGRATH: And that
definitely makes sense.

00:36:57.770 --> 00:37:00.260
There's definitely a number
of customers out there

00:37:00.260 --> 00:37:01.760
who do want to
continue to leverage

00:37:01.760 --> 00:37:03.290
their on-prem investments.

00:37:03.290 --> 00:37:04.070
FENG LU: Exactly.

00:37:04.070 --> 00:37:04.580
We see that.

00:37:04.580 --> 00:37:08.990
So we think like, using GKE
On-Prem might be a good bridge

00:37:08.990 --> 00:37:10.130
to connect it to.

00:37:10.130 --> 00:37:12.320
TOM MCGRATH: Definitely.

00:37:12.320 --> 00:37:15.050
FENG LU: We're also looking
at adding more operators.

00:37:15.050 --> 00:37:19.430
This year, we plan to add
another 20 or 30 GCP service

00:37:19.430 --> 00:37:21.592
related operators into Airflow.

00:37:21.592 --> 00:37:22.550
TOM MCGRATH: Very cool.

00:37:22.550 --> 00:37:24.770
FENG LU: And resource usage.

00:37:24.770 --> 00:37:27.900
We definitely want to see
more resource elasticity,

00:37:27.900 --> 00:37:29.660
ideally to the
state where if you

00:37:29.660 --> 00:37:32.329
don't have any
workflow executing,

00:37:32.329 --> 00:37:33.620
you don't have to pay anything.

00:37:33.620 --> 00:37:35.270
That would be our final goal.

00:37:35.270 --> 00:37:36.520
TOM MCGRATH: That makes sense.

00:37:36.520 --> 00:37:38.270
And obviously, you
constrain the amount

00:37:38.270 --> 00:37:41.300
needed or consumed based on
usage and based on needs.

00:37:41.300 --> 00:37:42.655
FENG LU: Exactly.

00:37:42.655 --> 00:37:44.780
TOM MCGRATH: Definitely
consistent with our managed

00:37:44.780 --> 00:37:45.445
services.

00:37:45.445 --> 00:37:46.070
FENG LU: Right.

00:37:46.070 --> 00:37:49.190
So with all that being said,
how should I get started today?

00:37:49.190 --> 00:37:51.940
There are a few places
you can get resources.

00:37:51.940 --> 00:37:54.920
Our Apache Airflow website
has organized tutorials

00:37:54.920 --> 00:37:56.510
and information
covering the basics.

00:37:56.510 --> 00:38:02.660
And some of the examples
are in the website.

00:38:02.660 --> 00:38:07.940
But also, we have a
lot more sample code,

00:38:07.940 --> 00:38:10.600
how-tos, and then
also the ways how

00:38:10.600 --> 00:38:14.080
to interconnect with
other GCP services

00:38:14.080 --> 00:38:17.160
in our Cloud Composer
Cloud doc website.

00:38:17.160 --> 00:38:18.890
TOM MCGRATH: Cool.

00:38:18.890 --> 00:38:22.175
FENG LU: With that,
that will be--

00:38:22.175 --> 00:38:23.300
TOM MCGRATH: That's a wrap.

00:38:23.300 --> 00:38:23.883
FENG LU: Yeah.

00:38:23.883 --> 00:38:25.240
TOM MCGRATH: That's a wrap.

00:38:25.240 --> 00:38:29.320
So stay tuned for live Q&amp;A,
and we will be back with you

00:38:29.320 --> 00:38:30.280
in less than a minute.

00:39:37.970 --> 00:39:38.630
OK.

00:39:38.630 --> 00:39:40.839
And we're back with some
questions from the audience.

00:39:40.839 --> 00:39:42.671
So I think one of the
first questions that's

00:39:42.671 --> 00:39:44.330
pretty interesting
is what should I

00:39:44.330 --> 00:39:46.720
consider when deciding
whether to use Cloud Composer

00:39:46.720 --> 00:39:47.575
or Dataflow?

00:39:47.575 --> 00:39:48.950
And I think one
of the key things

00:39:48.950 --> 00:39:52.610
there is to clarify
that Cloud Composer is

00:39:52.610 --> 00:39:56.510
really more about
orchestration and making things

00:39:56.510 --> 00:40:00.560
happen within various systems,
whereas Dataflow is more

00:40:00.560 --> 00:40:06.300
for processing and ETLing both
streaming and batch data moving

00:40:06.300 --> 00:40:09.620
into some sort of data
sink as necessary.

00:40:09.620 --> 00:40:14.000
But because of all those
operators within Airflow

00:40:14.000 --> 00:40:17.060
and available within
Cloud Composer,

00:40:17.060 --> 00:40:19.640
I can see it's very
easy to say, well, gosh,

00:40:19.640 --> 00:40:22.190
couldn't I use Cloud
Composer as an ETL tool?

00:40:22.190 --> 00:40:24.565
And that would probably not
be the best way to proceed.

00:40:24.565 --> 00:40:25.190
FENG LU: Right.

00:40:25.190 --> 00:40:27.170
And the way I would
also call this will be--

00:40:27.170 --> 00:40:31.562
Composer will be a control flow
orchestration system, where

00:40:31.562 --> 00:40:34.820
Dataflow is more--
obviously, very data centric,

00:40:34.820 --> 00:40:36.380
data processing focused.

00:40:36.380 --> 00:40:38.090
TOM MCGRATH: Yeah, definitely.

00:40:38.090 --> 00:40:39.830
And so another question
that we received

00:40:39.830 --> 00:40:41.420
is, what are some
of the reasons why

00:40:41.420 --> 00:40:44.240
it would be difficult
to run airflow myself

00:40:44.240 --> 00:40:47.980
and I should consider leveraging
cloud composer instead?

00:40:47.980 --> 00:40:51.920
And my understanding is
that when it comes time

00:40:51.920 --> 00:40:55.160
to install, and run it,
and things like that,

00:40:55.160 --> 00:40:57.220
that can take a lot of effort.

00:40:57.220 --> 00:40:58.720
FENG LU: Right.

00:40:58.720 --> 00:41:01.005
Try to create a [INAUDIBLE]
yourself and you

00:41:01.005 --> 00:41:02.140
will see all [INAUDIBLE].

00:41:02.140 --> 00:41:02.640
[LAUGHING]

00:41:02.640 --> 00:41:03.420
Think about it.

00:41:03.420 --> 00:41:06.800
You also need to
upgrade dependencies,

00:41:06.800 --> 00:41:09.500
especially if you try to
run Airflow in a distributed

00:41:09.500 --> 00:41:10.250
environment.

00:41:10.250 --> 00:41:12.950
Every time you try
to install a package,

00:41:12.950 --> 00:41:16.410
you have to make sure that it's
installing all your instances

00:41:16.410 --> 00:41:17.450
or services.

00:41:17.450 --> 00:41:18.530
TOM MCGRATH: Got it.

00:41:18.530 --> 00:41:20.113
FENG LU: And think
about how would you

00:41:20.113 --> 00:41:23.090
be able to consistently
distribute your DAGs to all

00:41:23.090 --> 00:41:24.540
your Airflow instances.

00:41:24.540 --> 00:41:24.920
TOM MCGRATH: Makes sense.

00:41:24.920 --> 00:41:27.045
FENG LU: Those are really
tricky and hard to manage

00:41:27.045 --> 00:41:28.790
by yourself problems.

00:41:28.790 --> 00:41:30.842
TOM MCGRATH: Big challenges.

00:41:30.842 --> 00:41:32.300
And how much effort
do you actually

00:41:32.300 --> 00:41:34.737
want to put into making those
things work is the question.

00:41:34.737 --> 00:41:35.320
FENG LU: Yeah.

00:41:35.320 --> 00:41:37.080
We really want you to
be able to say, hey,

00:41:37.080 --> 00:41:39.800
I want to use Airflow--

00:41:39.800 --> 00:41:42.240
10 minutes after that, you
can start using Airflow

00:41:42.240 --> 00:41:45.090
by writing your first DAG
without worrying about, hmm,

00:41:45.090 --> 00:41:47.550
how should I install my
Airflow on GCP or other cloud

00:41:47.550 --> 00:41:48.259
providers?

00:41:48.259 --> 00:41:49.300
TOM MCGRATH: Makes sense.

00:41:49.300 --> 00:41:50.860
And another interesting
one is there

00:41:50.860 --> 00:41:52.980
is a KubernetesPodOperator.

00:41:52.980 --> 00:41:54.525
That would work with
Kubeflow, right,

00:41:54.525 --> 00:41:56.767
for running ML models
and things like that?

00:41:56.767 --> 00:41:57.600
FENG LU: Absolutely.

00:41:57.600 --> 00:42:01.200
As I mentioned, there was the
GKE pod operator that also

00:42:01.200 --> 00:42:03.070
is the concern of resources.

00:42:03.070 --> 00:42:04.570
And you probably
don't have to force

00:42:04.570 --> 00:42:07.080
to run your container
in the same cluster

00:42:07.080 --> 00:42:08.970
where you run your
composing environment.

00:42:08.970 --> 00:42:12.460
You can launch your
task in any GKE cluster.

00:42:12.460 --> 00:42:14.340
Presumably some of the ones--

00:42:14.340 --> 00:42:16.530
you might already be using
those to run Kubeflows.

00:42:16.530 --> 00:42:17.040
TOM MCGRATH: Definitely.

00:42:17.040 --> 00:42:18.748
And something I think
that's specifically

00:42:18.748 --> 00:42:21.007
interesting with
Kubeflow is if you

00:42:21.007 --> 00:42:22.590
think of all the
data engineering that

00:42:22.590 --> 00:42:27.510
goes into creating
an ML model, I mean,

00:42:27.510 --> 00:42:29.460
Cloud Composer
becomes a natural fit

00:42:29.460 --> 00:42:32.310
to orchestrate those
tasks end to end.

00:42:32.310 --> 00:42:33.530
That's really awesome.

00:42:33.530 --> 00:42:34.860
FENG LU: Yeah.

00:42:34.860 --> 00:42:37.410
TOM MCGRATH: What other
questions do we have?

00:42:37.410 --> 00:42:39.660
Can I kick off a DAG
based on time of day

00:42:39.660 --> 00:42:42.190
or from a Cloud Function?

00:42:42.190 --> 00:42:43.640
FENG LU: Yes, I think.

00:42:43.640 --> 00:42:44.410
[LAUGHING]

00:42:44.410 --> 00:42:48.000
From a day, for sure, and
from a cloud function, yes.

00:42:48.000 --> 00:42:50.460
That's one thing I want to say--

00:42:50.460 --> 00:42:54.060
why we decided to host Airflow
web server in this way.

00:42:54.060 --> 00:42:57.540
Hosting it in [INAUDIBLE]
and also [INAUDIBLE]

00:42:57.540 --> 00:42:58.510
gives you two things.

00:42:58.510 --> 00:43:03.600
One is that Airflow web
UI is publicly accessible,

00:43:03.600 --> 00:43:07.850
but with a security
protection in front.

00:43:07.850 --> 00:43:11.430
So from Cloud function--

00:43:11.430 --> 00:43:15.750
in fact, we have a tutorial
on our Cloud doc website

00:43:15.750 --> 00:43:20.330
that talks about how you
can trigger a DAG execution,

00:43:20.330 --> 00:43:23.320
we'll have it as
a GCS file change.

00:43:23.320 --> 00:43:25.320
So it works like,
you have GCS change,

00:43:25.320 --> 00:43:29.180
your trigger a GCS call, and
that GCS call makes an HTTP

00:43:29.180 --> 00:43:31.009
POST request to the
composing environment,

00:43:31.009 --> 00:43:32.550
and then it starts
the DAG execution.

00:43:32.550 --> 00:43:33.500
TOM MCGRATH: Perfect.

00:43:33.500 --> 00:43:35.790
Maybe I have some
data landing in GCS,

00:43:35.790 --> 00:43:37.712
and then I want to import
that into BigQuery,

00:43:37.712 --> 00:43:39.420
and then do something
with it from there.

00:43:39.420 --> 00:43:40.045
FENG LU: Right.

00:43:40.045 --> 00:43:42.540
Also, I just want to tie that
with the templating things

00:43:42.540 --> 00:43:43.800
we have discussed.

00:43:43.800 --> 00:43:48.070
So with [INAUDIBLE] like
HTTP DAG triggering,

00:43:48.070 --> 00:43:51.680
you can also send along
parameters or configuration

00:43:51.680 --> 00:43:52.390
values.

00:43:52.390 --> 00:43:55.564
Those will all get instantiated
together with each DAG run.

00:43:55.564 --> 00:43:56.730
TOM MCGRATH: That's awesome.

00:43:56.730 --> 00:43:57.850
Really cool.

00:43:57.850 --> 00:44:02.880
And then really quickly with
the KubernetesPodOperator

00:44:02.880 --> 00:44:05.220
is it possible to
startup the cluster

00:44:05.220 --> 00:44:07.860
and then shut it down
when the sensor recognizes

00:44:07.860 --> 00:44:09.136
that the task is complete?

00:44:09.136 --> 00:44:11.010
FENG LU: Wow, that sounds
like a lot of work.

00:44:11.010 --> 00:44:12.400
[LAUGHING]

00:44:12.400 --> 00:44:15.270
I would say, yes, you can do it.

